## an4.38
an4.38:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a mendicant has cast aside idiosyncratic interpretations of the truth, has totally given up searching, has stilled the physical process, and is said to be ‘withdrawn’. | T: “Monks, a monk who has shaken off idiosyncratic truths [pacceka-sacca], who has thoroughly given up searching, who is calmed in his bodily fabrication, is said to be detached.
an4.38:1.2 | S: And how has a mendicant cast aside idiosyncratic interpretations of the truth? | T: “And how has a monk shaken off idiosyncratic truths?
an4.38:1.3 | S: Different ascetics and brahmins have different idiosyncratic interpretations of the truth. For example: | T: There is the case where a monk has shaken off the run-of-the-mill idiosyncratic truths of run-of-the-mill contemplatives & brahmans—in other words,
an4.38:1.4 | S: the cosmos is eternal, or not eternal, or finite, or infinite; the soul and the body are one and the same, or the soul is one thing, the body another; after death, a realized one still exists, or no longer exists, or both still exists and no longer exists, or neither still exists nor no longer exists. | T: ‘The cosmos is eternal,’ ‘The cosmos is not eternal,’ ‘The cosmos is finite,’ ‘The cosmos is infinite,’ ‘The soul & the body are the same,’ ‘The soul is one thing and the body another,’ ‘After death a Tathāgata exists,’ ‘After death a Tathāgata does not exist,’ ‘After death a Tathāgata both does & does not exist,’ ‘After death a Tathāgata neither does nor does not exist.’
an4.38:1.5 | S: A mendicant has cast out, cast aside, thrown out, discarded, let go of, given up, and relinquished all these. | T: All of these he has thrown off, shaken off, renounced, vomited up, let go, abandoned, relinquished.
an4.38:1.6 | S: That’s how a mendicant has cast aside idiosyncratic interpretations of the truth. | T: This is how a monk has shaken off idiosyncratic truths.
an4.38:2.1 | S: And how has a mendicant totally given up searching? | T: “And how has a monk thoroughly given up searching?
an4.38:2.2 | S: It’s when they’ve given up searching for sensual pleasures, for continued existence, and for a spiritual path. | T: There is the case where a monk has abandoned his search for sensuality, has abandoned his search for becoming, and has allayed his search for a holy life.
an4.38:2.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant has totally given up searching. | T: This is how a monk has thoroughly given up searching.
an4.38:3.1 | S: And how has a mendicant stilled the physical process? | T: “And how is a monk calmed in his bodily fabrication?
an4.38:3.2 | S: It’s when, with the giving up of pleasure and pain and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, they enter and remain in the fourth absorption, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and mindfulness. | T: There is the case where a monk, with the abandoning of pleasure & pain—as with the earlier disappearance of elation & distress—enters & remains in the fourth jhāna: purity of equanimity & mindfulness, neither pleasure nor pain.
an4.38:3.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant has stilled the physical process. | T: This is how a monk is calmed in his bodily fabrication.
an4.38:4.1 | S: And how is a mendicant withdrawn? | T: “And how is a monk detached?
an4.38:4.2 | S: It’s when they’ve given up the conceit ‘I am’, cut it off at the root, made it like a palm stump, obliterated it, so it’s unable to arise in the future. | T: There is the case where a monk’s conceit, ‘I am,’ is abandoned, its root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising.
an4.38:4.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is withdrawn. | T: This is how a monk is detached.”
an4.38:4.4 | S: A mendicant has cast aside idiosyncratic interpretations of the truth, has totally given up searching, has stilled the physical process, and is said to be ‘withdrawn’. | T: (none)
an4.38:5.1 | S: The search for sensual pleasures, <j>for continued existence, | T: Sensuality-search, becoming-search,
an4.38:5.2 | S: and the search for a spiritual path; | T: together with the holy-life search—
an4.38:5.3 | S: the holding tight to the thought, ‘this is the truth’, | T: i.e., grasping at ‘such is the truth’
an4.38:5.4 | S: and the mass of grounds for views— | T: based on an accumulation of viewpoints:
an4.38:6.1 | S: for one detached from all lusts, | T: Through the relinquishing of searches & the abolishing of viewpoints of one dispassionate to all passion,
an4.38:6.2 | S: freed by the ending of craving, | T: and released in the ending of craving,
an4.38:6.3 | S: that searching has been relinquished, | T: (none)
an4.38:6.4 | S: and those grounds for views eradicated. | T: (none)
an4.38:7.1 | S: That mendicant is peaceful and mindful, | T: he, a monk peaceful, mindful,
an4.38:7.2 | S: tranquil and undefeated. | T: calmed, undefeated,
an4.38:7.3 | S: And when they’re awakened <j>by comprehending conceit, | T: awakened with the breaking-through of conceit,
