## sn11.24
sn11.24:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn11.24:1.2 | S: Now at that time two mendicants had clashed. | T: And on that occasion two monks were quarreling, and one of them committed a transgression.
sn11.24:1.3 | S: One of them transgressed against the other. | T: (none)
sn11.24:1.4 | S: The transgressor confessed to the other mendicant, | T: So, in the presence of the other, he confessed his transgression as a transgression,
sn11.24:1.5 | S: but they didn’t accept it. | T: but the other didn’t pardon him.
sn11.24:1.6 | S: Then several mendicants went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. | T: So a large number of monks went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there, they said to the Blessed One,
sn11.24:1.7 | P: “idha, bhante, dve bhikkhū sampayojesuṁ, tatreko bhikkhu accasarā. | T: “Just now, lord, two monks were quarreling, and one of them committed a transgression.
sn11.24:1.8 | P: Atha kho so, bhante, bhikkhu tassa bhikkhuno santike accayaṁ accayato deseti, so bhikkhu nappaṭiggaṇhātī”ti. | T: So, in the presence of the other, he confessed his transgression as a transgression, but the other didn’t pardon him.
sn11.24:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are two fools. | T: “Monks, there are these two fools. Which two?
sn11.24:2.2 | S: One who doesn’t recognize when they’ve made a mistake. And one who doesn’t properly accept the confession of someone who’s made a mistake. | T: The one who doesn’t see his transgression as a transgression, and the one who doesn’t rightfully pardon another who has confessed his transgression.
sn11.24:2.3 | S: These are the two fools. | T: These two are fools.
sn11.24:2.4 | S: There are two who are astute. | T: “There are these two wise people. Which two?
sn11.24:2.5 | S: One who recognizes when they’ve made a mistake. And one who properly accepts the confession of someone who’s made a mistake. | T: The one who sees his transgression as a transgression, and the one who rightfully pardons another who has confessed his transgression.
sn11.24:2.6 | S: These are the two who are astute. | T: These two are wise people.
sn11.24:3.1 | S: Once upon a time, mendicants, Sakka, lord of gods, guiding the gods of the thirty-three, spoke this verse: | T: “Once, monks, Sakka the deva-king, having conciliated the devas of the Thirty-three in the Sudhamma assembly hall, on that occasion recited this verse:
sn11.24:4.1 | S: ‘Control your anger; | T: “‘Bring your anger under control.
sn11.24:4.2 | S: don’t let friendships decay. | T: Don’t let friendships decay.
sn11.24:4.3 | S: Don’t blame the blameless, | T: Don’t blame the blameless.
sn11.24:4.4 | S: and don’t backbite. | T: Don’t speak divisively,
sn11.24:4.5 | S: For anger crushes bad people | T: for anger, like a mountain avalanche, crushes evil people.’”
sn11.24:4.6 | S: like a mountain.’” | T: (none)

## an5.41
an5.41:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: (none)
an5.41:1.2 | S: Then the householder Anāthapiṇḍika went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him: | T: Then Anāthapiṇḍika the householder went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there the Blessed One said to him:
an5.41:1.3 | S: “Householder, there are these five reasons to get rich. | T: “There are these five benefits that can be obtained from wealth.
an5.41:1.4 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an5.41:1.5 | S: Firstly, with his legitimate wealth—earned by his efforts and initiative, built up with his own hands, gathered by the sweat of the brow—a noble disciple makes himself happy and pleased, keeping himself properly happy. | T: “There is the case where the disciple of the noble ones—using the wealth earned through his efforts & enterprise, amassed through the strength of his arm, and piled up through the sweat of his brow, righteous wealth righteously gained—provides himself with pleasure & satisfaction, and maintains that pleasure rightly.
an5.41:1.6 | S: He makes his mother and father happy … | T: He provides his mother & father with pleasure & satisfaction, and maintains that pleasure rightly.
an5.41:1.7 | S: He makes his children, partners, bondservants, workers, and staff happy … | T: He provides his children, his wife, his slaves, servants, & assistants with pleasure & satisfaction, and maintains that pleasure rightly.
an5.41:1.8 | S: This is the first reason to get rich. | T: This is the first benefit that can be obtained from wealth.
an5.41:2.1 | S: Furthermore, with his legitimate wealth he makes his friends and colleagues happy … | T: “And further, the disciple of the noble ones—using the wealth earned through his efforts & enterprise, amassed through the strength of his arm, and piled up through the sweat of his brow, righteous wealth righteously gained—provides his friends & associates with pleasure & satisfaction, and maintains that pleasure rightly.
an5.41:2.2 | S: This is the second reason to get rich. | T: This is the second benefit that can be obtained from wealth.
an5.41:3.1 | S: Furthermore, with his legitimate wealth he protects himself against losses from such things as fire, water, kings, bandits, or unloved heirs. | T: “And further, the disciple of the noble ones—using the wealth earned through his efforts & enterprise, amassed through the strength of his arm, and piled up through the sweat of his brow, righteous wealth righteously gained—wards off calamities
an5.41:3.2 | P: aggito vā udakato vā rājato vā corato vā appiyato vā dāyādato— | T: coming from fire, flood, kings, thieves, or hateful heirs,
an5.41:3.3 | S: He keeps himself safe. | T: and keeps himself safe.
an5.41:3.4 | S: This is the third reason to get rich. | T: This is the third benefit that can be obtained from wealth.
an5.41:4.1 | S: Furthermore, with his legitimate wealth he makes five spirit-offerings: | T: “And further, the disciple of the noble ones—using the wealth earned through his efforts & enterprise, amassed through the strength of his arm, and piled up through the sweat of his brow, righteous wealth righteously gained—performs the five oblations:
an5.41:4.2 | S: to relatives, guests, ancestors, king, and deities. | T: to relatives, guests, the dead, kings, & devas.
an5.41:4.3 | S: This is the fourth reason to get rich. | T: This is the fourth benefit that can be obtained from wealth.
an5.41:5.1 | S: Furthermore, with his legitimate wealth he establishes an uplifting religious donation for ascetics and brahmins—those who refrain from intoxication and negligence, are settled in patience and gentleness, and who tame, calm, and extinguish themselves—that’s conducive to heaven, ripens in happiness, and leads to heaven. | T: “And further, the disciple of the noble ones—using the wealth earned through his efforts & enterprise, amassed through the strength of his arm, and piled up through the sweat of his brow, righteous wealth righteously gained—institutes offerings of supreme aim, heavenly, resulting in happiness, leading to heaven, given to contemplatives & brahmans who abstain from intoxication & heedlessness, who endure all things with patience & humility, each taming himself, each restraining himself, each taking himself to unbinding.
an5.41:5.2 | S: This is the fifth reason to get rich. | T: This is the fifth benefit that can be obtained from wealth.
an5.41:5.3 | S: These are the five reasons to get rich. | T: (none)
an5.41:6.1 | S: Now if the riches a noble disciple gets for these five reasons run out, he thinks: | T: “If it so happens that, when a disciple of the noble ones obtains these five benefits from wealth, his wealth goes to depletion, the thought occurs to him,
an5.41:6.2 | S: ‘So, the riches I have obtained for these reasons are running out.’ | T: ‘Even though my wealth has gone to depletion, I have obtained the five benefits that can be obtained from wealth,’
an5.41:6.3 | S: And so he has no regrets. | T: and he feels no remorse.
an5.41:6.4 | S: But if the riches a noble disciple gets for these five reasons increase, he thinks: | T: If it so happens that, when a disciple of the noble ones obtains these five benefits from wealth, his wealth increases, the thought occurs to him,
an5.41:6.5 | S: ‘So, the riches I have obtained for these reasons are increasing.’ | T: ‘I have obtained the five benefits that can be obtained from wealth, and my wealth has increased,’
an5.41:6.6 | S: And so he has no regrets in both cases. | T: and he feels no remorse. So he feels no remorse in either case.”
an5.41:7.1 | S: ‘I’ve enjoyed my wealth, <j>supporting those who depend on me; | T: ‘My wealth has been enjoyed, my dependents supported,
an5.41:7.2 | S: I’ve overcome losses; | T: protected from calamities by me.
an5.41:7.3 | S: I’ve given uplifting religious donations; | T: I have given supreme offerings
an5.41:7.4 | S: and made the five spirit-offerings. | T: & performed the five oblations.
an5.41:7.5 | S: I have looked after the ethical and | T: I have provided for the virtuous,
an5.41:7.6 | S: disciplined spiritual practitioners. | T: the restrained, followers of the holy life.
an5.41:8.1 | S: I’ve achieved the goal | T: For whatever aim a wise householder would desire wealth, that aim have I attained.
an5.41:8.2 | S: for which an astute layperson | T: (none)
an5.41:8.3 | S: wishes to gain wealth. | T: (none)
an5.41:8.4 | S: I don’t regret what I’ve done.’ | T: I have done what will not lead to future distress.’
an5.41:9.1 | S: A mortal man who recollects this | T: When this is recollected by a mortal,
an5.41:9.2 | S: stands firm in the teaching of the noble ones. | T: a person established in the Dhamma of the noble ones,
an5.41:9.3 | S: They’re praised in this life by the astute, | T: he is praised in this life
an5.41:9.4 | S: and they depart to rejoice in heaven.” | T: and, after death, rejoices in heaven.
an5.41:9.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn10.8
sn10.8:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Cool Grove. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha in the Cool Forest.
sn10.8:1.2 | S: Now at that time the householder Anāthapiṇḍika had arrived at Rājagaha on some business. | T: Now at that time Anāthapiṇḍika the householder had arrived in Rājagaha on some business.
sn10.8:1.3 | S: He heard | T: He heard,
sn10.8:1.4 | S: a rumor that a Buddha had arisen in the world. | T: “An Awakened One, they say, has appeared in the world,”
sn10.8:1.5 | S: Right away he wanted to go and see the Buddha, but he thought, | T: and he wanted to go right then to see the Blessed One. Then the thought occurred to him,
sn10.8:1.6 | S: “It’s too late to go and see the Buddha today. | T: “Today is not the proper time to go to see the Blessed One.
sn10.8:1.7 | S: I’ll go and see him tomorrow.” He went to bed thinking of the Buddha. | T: Tomorrow I will go to see the Blessed One at the proper time.” With his mindfulness immersed in the Awakened One he lay down to sleep.
sn10.8:1.8 | S: During the night he got up three times thinking it was sunup. | T: Three times he got up during the night, thinking it was light.
sn10.8:1.9 | S: Then he approached the Sivaka Gate, | T: Then he went to the gate to the charnel ground.
sn10.8:1.10 | S: and non-human beings opened it for him. | T: Non-human beings opened the gate.
sn10.8:1.11 | S: But as he was leaving the city, light vanished and darkness appeared to him. He felt fear, terror, and goosebumps, and wanted to turn back. | T: When Anāthapiṇḍika the householder had left the city, the light vanished and darkness appeared. Fear, terror, & horripilation arose, and because of that he wanted to turn back.
sn10.8:1.12 | S: Then the invisible spirit Sivaka called out: | T: Then Sivaka the yakkha-spirit, invisible, proclaimed:
sn10.8:2.1 | S: “A hundred elephants, a hundred horses, | T: “A hundred elephants, a hundred horses,
sn10.8:2.2 | S: a hundred mule-drawn chariots, | T: a hundred mule-drawn carts,
sn10.8:2.3 | S: a hundred thousand maidens | T: a hundred-thousand maidens
sn10.8:2.4 | S: bedecked with jeweled earrings: | T: adorned with jewels & earrings
sn10.8:2.5 | S: these are not worth a sixteenth part | T: aren’t worth one-sixteenth
sn10.8:2.6 | S: of a single forward stride! | T: of one step forward.
sn10.8:3.1 | S: Forward, householder! | T: Go forward, householder!
sn10.8:3.2 | S: Forward, householder! | T: Go forward, householder!
sn10.8:3.3 | S: Going forward is better for you, | T: Going forward is better for you,
sn10.8:3.4 | S: not turning back!” | T: not back!”
sn10.8:4.1 | S: Then darkness vanished and light appeared to Anāthapiṇḍika. His fear, terror, and goosebumps settled down. | T: The darkness then vanished for Anāthapiṇḍika and the light appeared. The fear, terror, & horripilation he had felt subsided.
sn10.8:4.2 | S: But for a second time, light vanished and darkness appeared to him. … | T: For a second time…
sn10.8:4.3 | S: For a second time the invisible spirit Sivaka called out … | T: (none)
sn10.8:5.1 | P: “Sataṁ hatthī sataṁ assā, | T: (none)
sn10.8:5.2 | P: …pe… | T: (none)
sn10.8:5.3 | P: Kalaṁ nāgghanti soḷasiṁ. | T: (none)
sn10.8:6.1 | P: Abhikkama gahapati, | T: (none)
sn10.8:6.2 | P: abhikkama gahapati; | T: (none)
sn10.8:6.3 | S: “… Going forward is better for you, | T: (none)
sn10.8:6.4 | S: not turning back!” | T: (none)
sn10.8:7.1 | S: Then darkness vanished and light appeared to Anāthapiṇḍika. His fear, terror, and goosebumps settled down. | T: (none)
sn10.8:7.2 | S: But for a third time, light vanished and darkness appeared to him. … | T: a third time, the light vanished and darkness appeared. Fear, terror, & horripilation arose, and because of that Anāthapiṇḍika wanted to turn back.
sn10.8:7.3 | S: For a third time the invisible spirit Sivaka called out … | T: Then for a third time, Sivaka the yakkha-spirit, invisible, proclaimed:
sn10.8:8.1 | P: “Sataṁ hatthī sataṁ assā, | T: “A hundred elephants, a hundred horses, a hundred mule-drawn carts, a hundred-thousand maidens
sn10.8:8.2 | P: …pe… | T: adorned with jewels & earrings
sn10.8:8.3 | P: Kalaṁ nāgghanti soḷasiṁ. | T: aren’t worth one-sixteenth of one step forward.
sn10.8:9.1 | P: Abhikkama gahapati, | T: Go forward, householder!
sn10.8:9.2 | P: abhikkama gahapati; | T: Go forward, householder!
sn10.8:9.3 | S: “… Going forward is better for you, | T: Going forward is better for you,
sn10.8:9.4 | S: not turning back!” | T: not back!”
sn10.8:10.1 | S: Then darkness vanished and light appeared to Anāthapiṇḍika. His fear, terror, and goosebumps settled down. | T: The darkness then vanished for Anāthapiṇḍika and the light appeared. The fear, terror, & horripilation he had felt subsided.
sn10.8:10.2 | S: Then the householder Anāthapiṇḍika went to the Cool Grove and approached the Buddha. | T: So Anāthapiṇḍika went to the Cool Forest.
sn10.8:11.1 | S: Now at that time the Buddha had risen at the crack of dawn and was walking mindfully in the open. | T: Now at that time, the Blessed One—having gotten up as the night was ending—was pacing back & forth in the open air.
sn10.8:11.2 | S: He saw Anāthapiṇḍika coming off in the distance. | T: He saw Anāthapiṇḍika the householder coming from afar.
sn10.8:11.3 | S: So he stepped down from the walking path, sat down on the seat spread out, | T: On seeing him, he got down from his meditation path and sat on a seat made ready.
sn10.8:11.4 | S: and said to Anāthapiṇḍika, | T: As he was sitting there he said to Anāthapiṇḍika,
sn10.8:11.5 | S: “Come, Sudatta.” | T: “Come, Sudatta.”
sn10.8:11.6 | S: Then Anāthapiṇḍika thought, “The Buddha calls me by name!” Smiling and elated, he bowed with his head to the Buddha’s feet and said to him, | T: Then Anāthapiṇḍika, (thinking,) “The Blessed One is calling me by my given name!” threw himself down right there at the Blessed One’s feet and said to him,
sn10.8:11.7 | S: “Sir, I trust the Buddha slept well?” | T: “Lord, I hope the Blessed One has slept in ease.”
sn10.8:12.1 | S: “A brahmin who is fully quenched | T: The Buddha: “Always, always, he sleeps in ease: the brahman totally unbound,
sn10.8:12.2 | S: always sleeps at ease. | T: (none)
sn10.8:12.3 | S: Sensual pleasures slip off them, | T: who doesn’t adhere to sensual pleasures,
sn10.8:12.4 | S: they’re cooled, free of attachments. | T: who’s without acquisitions & cooled.
sn10.8:13.1 | S: Since they’ve cut off all clinging, | T: Having cut all ties
sn10.8:13.2 | S: and removed the stress from the heart, | T: & subdued fear in the heart,
sn10.8:13.3 | S: the peaceful sleep at ease, | T: calmed, he sleeps in ease,
sn10.8:13.4 | S: having found peace of mind.” | T: having reached peace of awareness.”

## an10.118
an10.118:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you the near shore and the far shore. | T: “Monks, I will teach you the near shore & the far shore.
an10.118:1.2 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
an10.118:1.3 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to the Blessed One.
an10.118:1.4 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
an10.118:2.1 | S: “And what, mendicants, is the near shore? What is the far shore? | T: “And which is the near shore? And which is the far shore?
an10.118:2.2 | S: Wrong view is the near shore, and right view is the far shore. … | T: “Wrong view is the near shore; right view, the far shore. “Wrong resolve is the near shore; right resolve, the far shore. “Wrong speech is the near shore; right speech, the far shore. “Wrong action is the near shore; right action, the far shore. “Wrong livelihood is the near shore; right livelihood, the far shore. “Wrong effort is the near shore; right effort, the far shore. “Wrong mindfulness is the near shore; right mindfulness, the far shore. “Wrong concentration is the near shore; right concentration, the far shore. “Wrong knowledge is the near shore; right knowledge, the far shore.
an10.118:2.3 | S: Wrong freedom is the near shore, and right freedom is the far shore. | T: “Wrong release is the near shore; right release, the far shore.
an10.118:2.4 | S: This is the near shore, and this is the far shore. | T: “This, monks, is the near shore; this, the far shore.”
